      <p>
        Of all the conflicts examined in this report, none has produced a more total, documented destruction of an education system than the war in Gaza. The term that has emerged among UN experts, human rights scholars, and educators is <strong>"scholasticide"</strong> — the systematic obliteration of a society's entire educational infrastructure.
      </p>

      <p>
        When the conflict began on October 7, 2023, Gaza had 19 universities, hundreds of schools, and over 625,000 enrolled students. By spring 2025, all of that had been dismantled. Not damaged — dismantled.
      </p>

      <!-- Gaza war card -->
      <div class="war-card">
        <div class="war-card-header">
          <div class="flag-emoji">🇵🇸</div>
          <div class="war-title-block">
            <div class="war-name">Gaza — Israel-Hamas War</div>
            <div class="war-period">October 2023 – Present · Source: UN OHCHR, UNICEF, Al Mezan, Ministry of Education</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="war-card-body">
          <div class="card-stats">
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num red-num">12,800+</span><span class="cs-label">Students killed</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num amber-num">760</span><span class="cs-label">Teachers & staff killed</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num blue-num">150</span><span class="cs-label">Academics & researchers killed</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num red-num">19 / 19</span><span class="cs-label">Universities destroyed</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num amber-num">778 / 815</span><span class="cs-label">Schools damaged or destroyed</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num blue-num">625,000+</span><span class="cs-label">Students with no access to education</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>
            <strong>By the numbers:</strong> According to the Palestinian Ministry of Education and Al Mezan Center for Human Rights, at least 12,800 students have been killed and over 16,700 injured since October 7, 2023. All 19 universities in Gaza — including Al-Azhar, the Islamic University, the University of Palestine, and Al-Aqsa University — have been either severely damaged or completely demolished.
          </p>
          <p>
            Israa University, the last functioning university in Gaza, was demolished by Israeli forces on January 17, 2024. A video of its controlled explosion was released by Israeli media. UNESCO estimates that over 95% of higher education campuses across Gaza have been severely damaged or destroyed. The Islamic University alone reported the loss of 16 buildings, its central library, over 240,000 books, 8,000 periodicals, and more than 16,000 master's and doctoral theses — a loss valued at approximately $141.9 million.
          </p>
          <p>
            As of early 2026, <strong>750,000 Palestinian students have gone without formal schooling for two consecutive academic years</strong>. Researchers at the University of Cambridge and the Centre for Lebanese Studies estimate that Israel's attacks have set back Palestinian children's education by five years, risking the creation of a "lost generation."
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Testimony 1 -->
      <div class="testimony">
        <div class="testimony-name">Mohammed — Software Engineering Student, University of Palestine, Gaza</div>
        <p class="testimony-text">
          "Before the assault, I was working with my friends on our graduation project. I dreamed of becoming a prominent software engineer. Now I run a humble stall to help my family. All 10 of us live in a 4×4 tent. My last hope was crushed when the Rafah crossing suddenly shut down."
        </p>
      </div>

      <!-- Testimony 2 -->
      <div class="testimony">
        <div class="testimony-name">Dalia & Sally Ibed — Twin Engineers, Accepted to University of Waterloo, Canada</div>
        <p class="testimony-text">
          Dalia and Sally, 26-year-old twin sisters, had been accepted to a mechatronics engineering programme in Canada with full scholarships. They were in their room in northern Gaza studying when two tank shells struck their building. Both sisters, their mother, and two siblings were killed — while their Canadian student visas were still being processed.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">⚠ Documented Pattern</div>
        <p>UN experts have noted that over 200 Israeli attacks on educational facilities have been recorded since October 2023. In July 2024, the UNRWA spokesperson stated that strikes on schools had become "almost a daily occurrence." Events like the October 2024 Asmaa School airstrike — which killed at least 73 people — illustrate the scale of casualties in schools being used as displacement shelters.</p>
      </div>

      <!-- International students stuck -->
      <h3>International Students Denied Escape</h3>
      <p>
        The tragedy is compounded for those accepted to study abroad. According to the group Palestinian Students and Scholars At Risk (PSSAR), over 80 Gazan students received full scholarships to pursue master's and PhD programmes in Canada during the war — and were still awaiting visas as of late 2025. Many waited over a year with no update. Some, like Sakher Yousef, lost their entire families while waiting. Others, like the Ibed twins, never survived to receive their visas.
      </p>

      <!-- YEMEN -->
      <h2 id="yemen">Yemen: A Decade of Classroom Destruction</h2>

      <img
        class="sec-img"
        src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1503676260728-1c00da094a0b?w=900&q=80"
        alt="Children studying in makeshift conditions representing Yemen's education crisis"
      />
      <p class="caption">↑ Millions of Yemeni students have known only wartime schooling — or none at all. (Photo: Unsplash / Illustrative)</p>

      <p>
        Yemen's education crisis predates Gaza's by nearly a decade. When the Saudi-led coalition military intervention began in March 2015, 3,600 schools immediately closed, throwing over 1.8 million additional students out of education and adding them to the 1.6 million who were already out of school. Today, a full decade into the conflict, the damage is generational.
      </p>

      <div class="war-card">
        <div class="war-card-header">
          <div class="flag-emoji">🇾🇪</div>
          <div class="war-title-block">
            <div class="war-name">Yemen Civil War</div>
            <div class="war-period">2014 – Present · Source: UNICEF, ICRC, Global Partnership for Education, Save the Children</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="war-card-body">
          <div class="card-stats">
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num red-num">4.5M</span><span class="cs-label">Children currently out of school</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num amber-num">2,900+</span><span class="cs-label">Schools destroyed or damaged</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num blue-num">170,000</span><span class="cs-label">Teachers unpaid for years</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num red-num">130</span><span class="cs-label">University attacks (2015–2019)</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num amber-num">40%</span><span class="cs-label">of school-age children out of school</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num blue-num">2,000+</span><span class="cs-label">Child soldiers killed (Jan 2020–May 2021)</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>
            As of early 2024, around <strong>4.5 million children — roughly 40% of Yemen's school-age population</strong> — are out of school. This is double the number from 2015, when the conflict escalated. UNICEF warns the figure could rise further without urgent intervention.
          </p>
          <p>
            Yemen also recorded the highest number of attacks on higher education facilities in the world between 2015 and 2019, with 130 attacks on university campuses alone, according to GCPEA's "Education Under Attack 2020" report. On August 9, 2018, a school bus carrying children on a field trip in northern Yemen was struck by an airstrike — killing at least 51 people. The Saudi-led coalition later admitted responsibility. On Mosul University's grounds in Iraq, an unexploded device detonated on April 6, 2017 while students were cleaning the building — killing one student and wounding four others.
          </p>
          <p>
            The teacher salary crisis has compounded everything. Two-thirds of Yemen's teachers — nearly 172,000 people — have gone without regular pay since 2016. Schools have been converted into military barracks, shelters for displaced families, and even Houthi recruitment camps where children as young as 12 are indoctrinated.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="fquote">
        <p class="fquote-text">"One time, my classmate was shot dead on our way to school. I was with him and it was horrific. I was covered in his blood and didn't know what to do."</p>
        <div class="fquote-src">Ahmed, 17 — Student from Taiz, Yemen · ICRC</div>
      </div>

      <!-- SYRIA -->
      <h2 id="syria">Syria: Over a Million Displaced Students</h2>

      <div class="war-card">
        <div class="war-card-header">
          <div class="flag-emoji">🇸🇾</div>
          <div class="war-title-block">
            <div class="war-name">Syrian Civil War</div>
            <div class="war-period">2011 – Present · Source: UNESCO, UNHCR, IIE, World Bank</div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="war-card-body">
          <div class="card-stats">
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num red-num">6M+</span><span class="cs-label">Syrians displaced internationally</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num amber-num">500–999</span><span class="cs-label">Reported attacks on schools (2015–19)</span></div>
            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num blue-num">79%</span><span class="cs-label">of surveyed students couldn't read at grade level</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>
            The Syrian civil war, now entering its 15th year, has displaced over 6 million people internationally — many of them students and young people who have spent formative years in refugee camps in Turkey, Lebanon, Jordan, and Egypt. The Institute of International Education (IIE) found that the <strong>overwhelming majority of Syrian university-age refugees in Lebanon, particularly women, were not continuing any form of higher education</strong> — facing legal barriers, discrimination, and security threats in host countries.
          </p>
          <p>
            An IRC survey of students in Idlib found that 79% of 4th, 5th, and 6th graders were unable to read second-grade material, and 71% could not perform basic math. The Syrian war's education losses have been described by researchers as setting an entire generation back by multiple academic years — with the damage compounding every year the conflict continues.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- IRAQ -->
      <h2 id="iraq">Iraq: Universities Caught in the ISIS War</h2>

      <div class="war-card">
        <div class="war-card-header">
