            <div class="cs"><span class="cs-num blue-num">Mosul U.</span><span class="cs-label">Major historic university — severely damaged by ISIS</span></div>
          </div>
          <p>
            Mosul University, one of Iraq's most historic academic institutions, was severely damaged during the fighting to retake the city from the Islamic State in 2016–2017. After the defeat of ISIS, unexploded ordnance left on educational premises continued to claim lives. On April 6, 2017, an explosive device detonated inside Mosul University while a group of students was cleaning a building, <strong>killing one student and wounding four others</strong>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Brown University's Costs of War project estimates that across Iraq, Afghanistan, Syria, Yemen, and Pakistan combined, over 940,000 people have been killed by direct post-9/11 war violence since 2001, with civilians accounting for more than 432,000 of those deaths — a figure that includes teachers, students, and university staff.
          </p>
