          <p>
            Lebanon's education system was already in crisis before the latest military escalation. Over the past six years, Lebanese children have lost up to <strong>60% of normal school time</strong> due to compounding disasters: an influx of Palestinian and Syrian refugees, Lebanon's severe financial collapse, the 2020 Beirut port explosion, COVID-19, and now renewed hostilities. A University of Cambridge report released in late 2024 described the situation as having turned "a decade of education crisis into a catastrophe."
          </p>
          <p>
            As of March 2026, UNICEF reports that renewed military escalation has displaced 91,000 people in Lebanon — including 9,000 children — in a single day, following Israeli airstrikes. Schools are once again being used as emergency shelters. Reports from schools in Iran have documented scores of students killed and injured in a single weekend of strikes, prompting fresh calls for de-escalation from the UN Secretary-General.
          </p>
          <p>
            Palestinian and Syrian refugee children inside Lebanon remain disproportionately affected — they were already the most marginalised before hostilities escalated. Syrian parents surveyed by Cambridge researchers were statistically more likely to consider education a high priority, despite being the least supported by official government plans.
          </p>
