      <!-- WHAT HAPPENS TO SURVIVORS -->
      <h2>What Happens to Students Who Survive?</h2>
      <p>
        Survival does not mean recovery. The students who live through these conflicts carry physical, psychological, and academic losses that compound over time. More than <strong>1 million Palestinian children in Gaza need mental health and psychosocial support</strong>, according to UN experts — and these are children who have known nothing but war for their entire adolescent lives.
      </p>
      <p>
        In Gaza, 17,000 students dropped out of studies at the Islamic University alone between 2023 and 2024. Those who attempted to continue online faced power cuts lasting 20+ hours a day, intermittent internet access, no equipment, and the constant psychological weight of displacement and loss. As Samah, a 21-year-old translation student, described it: studying online felt like "a desperate attempt to keep learning despite everything — the internet was weak, and I'd lose time reconnecting."
      </p>
      <p>
        In Yemen, child labour has surged as families lose income and stability. Girls face compounding risks: two-thirds of Yemeni girls are married off before the age of 18, a rate that increased sharply after 2015. Schools that should protect children are instead becoming sites of militarisation — with the Houthis using educational institutions to recruit child soldiers and run ideological indoctrination programmes.
      </p>

      <div class="fquote">
        <p class="fquote-text">"My only dream now is to survive, finish my studies, and help rebuild Gaza."</p>
        <div class="fquote-src">Fatima Skaik — Third-year Architecture Student, Islamic University of Gaza · The Intercept, 2025</div>
      </div>

      <!-- FAQ -->
      <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">What does "scholasticide" mean?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">The term, used formally by UN experts in April 2024, refers to the systemic obliteration of an education system — through the killing of teachers and students, destruction of educational infrastructure, and elimination of access to learning. It was applied specifically to describe the documented pattern of attacks on Gaza's schools, universities, and academic staff.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">How many Gaza students have lost their lives?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">According to Al Mezan Center for Human Rights (January 2025), at least 12,800 students have been killed since October 7, 2023. The Palestinian Ministry of Education had documented 10,490 student deaths as of September 2024. These figures continue to rise. Additionally, 760 teachers and educational staff and 150 academics and researchers have been killed.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Are international students also affected?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Yes. Students accepted to universities abroad — in Canada, the UK, the EU, and elsewhere — have been killed while waiting for visa processing. The group Palestinian Students and Scholars At Risk (PSSAR) documented over 80 students who received full scholarships to Canadian universities but remained stranded in Gaza pending security clearances with no timeline given. Some were killed before their visas were approved.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">Which Middle East conflict has caused the most education disruption?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">By raw scale of education infrastructure destruction, Gaza's war since 2023 has been the most concentrated and total in modern history — destroying 95% of school buildings and all 19 universities within 18 months. By total student numbers affected over time, Yemen's decade-long civil war has displaced and denied education to the greatest number of students: 4.5 million children currently out of school.</div>
      </div>
      <div class="faq-item">
        <div class="faq-q">What can students in other countries do to help?</div>
        <div class="faq-a">Several verified initiatives offer direct support: UNRWA's emergency education fund, the Scholars at Risk network (which advocates for academics in danger), and organisations like PSSAR, which specifically supports Palestinian students seeking to continue their education internationally. Raising awareness, contacting elected representatives about visa bottlenecks, and supporting verified humanitarian organisations are the most direct actions available.</div>
      </div>

      <!-- CONCLUSION -->
      <div class="conclusion">
        <h2>The Right to Learn Is Not a Privilege</h2>
        <p>
          Every university in Gaza has been destroyed. Four and a half million children in Yemen have no school to attend. Two million Syrian students are living in refugee camps across the Middle East, most of them without access to any form of higher education. In Lebanon, a generation has already lost 60% of its schooling years before the latest escalation began.
        </p>
        <p>
          These are not statistics to scroll past. Behind every number is a student who had a subject they loved, an exam they were preparing for, a career they had mapped out. The destruction of education is not collateral damage — in many of these conflicts, it is documented, deliberate, and systematic.
        </p>
        <p>
          At Student Toolkit, we believe in education as a fundamental right, not a privilege. Part of taking that belief seriously means acknowledging what happens to students when the structures that protect that right are destroyed. This is what it looks like when they are.
        </p>
      </div>

